  "metadata": {
    "title": "Trump Handed Intel Stock a 10% Pop, but Markets Are Hedging",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "Intel stock jumped about 10% after President Trump said Apple will make chips with the company. The surge pushed Intel (INTC) past a ceiling it had failed to clear twice.\nThe breakout looks promising on the chart. But money flow, crypto traders, and the options market each tell a more cautious story underneath.\nWhy Intel Stock Gapped Up on the Apple News\nIntel Corporation (INTC) gapped higher on Thursday. President Trump posted on Truth Social that Apple (AAPL) agreed to design and build chips in the US.\nHowever, neither company has formally confirmed the deal at press time. The caveat matters because Washington owns a piece of Intel. The US government bought about 10% of the company in August 2025.\nThe move caps a strong run. Intel stock has roughly tripled in 2026, helped by ties with Nvidia (NVDA) and Tesla (TSLA). Demand from Agentic AI, software that acts on its own, has also lifted sales of Intel's chips.\nRisks still linger. Intel's foundry arm stays unprofitable, and the PC market faces headwinds.\nThe INTC chart tells the first part of that story.\nINTC Breaks a Ceiling That Capped It Twice\nThe rally cleared $132.70, a level that had blocked Intel twice. That kind of pattern is a double top, where price stalls at the same high two times.\nINTC stock broke above it with force. Thursday's 233.91 million shares topped the volume behind the late-May push to the same area.\nMoney flow is turning, too. The Chaikin Money Flow (CMF), a gauge of institutional buying and selling pressure, climbed back to zero from negative territory. The recovery suggests selling has eased and larger buyers may be stepping back in.\nHowever, CMF sits at neutral, not clearly positive. So the buying interest is not yet confirmed. That's one market remaining cautious.\nPrice and volume lean bullish, yet positioning tells another story, starting with crypto traders.\nCrypto Traders Are Still Betting Against Intel\nCrypto desks are not buying the breakout yet. On Hyperliquid, an exchange that offers perpetual futures on stocks, smart money stays net short Intel. Perpetual futures are contracts that track a price with no expiry date.\nNansen data shows $7.41 million in shorts against $2.90 million in longs. That leaves a net short of $4.51 million across 21 wallets.\nStill, the bet against Intel is smaller than the crowd's shorts on Nvidia and Micron. Intel's long-to-short ratio sits at 0.39. That balance of bullish to bearish bets ranks among the least bearish in the group.\nIt is also rising, which suggests some traders are trimming shorts after the Apple news. Even so, the group has not flipped to net long.",
    "text_blocks": [
      "Intel stock jumped about 10% after President Trump said Apple will make chips with the company. The surge pushed Intel (INTC) past a ceiling it had failed to clear twice.",
      "The breakout looks promising on the chart. But money flow, crypto traders, and the options market each tell a more cautious story underneath.",
      "Why Intel Stock Gapped Up on the Apple News",
      "Intel Corporation (INTC) gapped higher on Thursday. President Trump posted on Truth Social that Apple (AAPL) agreed to design and build chips in the US.",
      "However, neither company has formally confirmed the deal at press time. The caveat matters because Washington owns a piece of Intel. The US government bought about 10% of the company in August 2025.",
      "The move caps a strong run. Intel stock has roughly tripled in 2026, helped by ties with Nvidia (NVDA) and Tesla (TSLA). Demand from Agentic AI, software that acts on its own, has also lifted sales of Intel's chips.",
      "Risks still linger. Intel's foundry arm stays unprofitable, and the PC market faces headwinds.",
      "The INTC chart tells the first part of that story.",
      "INTC Breaks a Ceiling That Capped It Twice",
      "The rally cleared $132.70, a level that had blocked Intel twice. That kind of pattern is a double top, where price stalls at the same high two times.",
      "INTC stock broke above it with force. Thursday's 233.91 million shares topped the volume behind the late-May push to the same area.",
      "Money flow is turning, too. The Chaikin Money Flow (CMF), a gauge of institutional buying and selling pressure, climbed back to zero from negative territory. The recovery suggests selling has eased and larger buyers may be stepping back in.",
      "However, CMF sits at neutral, not clearly positive. So the buying interest is not yet confirmed. That's one market remaining cautious.",
      "Price and volume lean bullish, yet positioning tells another story, starting with crypto traders.",
      "Crypto Traders Are Still Betting Against Intel",
      "Crypto desks are not buying the breakout yet. On Hyperliquid, an exchange that offers perpetual futures on stocks, smart money stays net short Intel. Perpetual futures are contracts that track a price with no expiry date.",
      "Nansen data shows $7.41 million in shorts against $2.90 million in longs. That leaves a net short of $4.51 million across 21 wallets.",
      "Still, the bet against Intel is smaller than the crowd's shorts on Nvidia and Micron. Intel's long-to-short ratio sits at 0.39. That balance of bullish to bearish bets ranks among the least bearish in the group.",
      "It is also rising, which suggests some traders are trimming shorts after the Apple news. Even so, the group has not flipped to net long."
    ],
